{"ts": 1789770707.190706, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "All signs confirmed (flipping any degrades sharply). **{27, 17, 35}** all-long at **1.142 IS** is the clear winner.\n\nNow the key question: what OOS Sharpe should I predict? I should not just report 1.142 \u2014 that number is biased upward by selection.\n\n**Sizing the selection bias.** I ran ~85 evaluations and picked the max. But the overfitting here is milder than the raw count suggests:\n- The winner wasn't a fluke assembly of noise \u2014 it's built from the three strongest standalone features (27: 0.871, 17: 0.577, 35: 0.528), each individually significant. With n=5000 daily obs (~20 yrs), the standard error on a Sharpe estimate is roughly \u221a(1/n) annualized \u2248 0.014\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22... more precisely SE(SR_annual) \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. Individual Sharpes of 0.5\u20130.9 are several SE above zero, so these are real signals, not noise.\n- The gain from combining (0.871 \u2192 1.142) is consistent with genuine diversification across imperfectly correlated real signals, not with noise-mining.\n\n**Sources of OOS shrinkage I still expect:**\n1. Selection bias from maximizing over ~85 specs: with SE \u2248 0.22, the max of many correlated draws is inflated. Most of my candidates were highly correlated (sharing 27 and 17), so the effective number of independent tries is maybe 10\u201320, implying inflation on the order of 0.15\u20130.30.\n2. Non-stationarity: OOS is a different regime; feature efficacy typically decays beyond pure sampling error. This is usually the bigger effect in practice.\n3. Estimation error in the signs/weights is minimal here (equal-weight, signs unambiguous).\n\nNetting these, I'll center my belief meaningfully below the IS value \u2014 around **0.80** \u2014 and use a wide SD of **0.30** to reflect genuine regime uncertainty. That SD spans roughly 0.2 to 1.4, which honestly covers both a substantial-decay scenario and a holds-up scenario."}
