{"ts": 1789770766.833686, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** Long features **27, 17, 35** (equal weight), in-sample Sharpe **1.142**.\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** mean **0.80**, SD **0.30**.\n\n### Process\n1. **Scanned all 40 features individually.** Strongest longs: 27 (0.871), 17 (0.577), 35 (0.528), 24 (0.427). Strongest shorts (sign-flipped): 26 (+0.411), 31 (+0.347), 20 (+0.294), 7 (+0.285).\n2. **Pair search** over the top candidates. Sharpes combined roughly as independent signals would (27+17 \u2192 1.020 from 0.871/0.577), indicating low cross-correlation and real diversification.\n3. **Triple search**, including a deliberate sweep of 18 *weak* features against the strong 27+17 core to check for sleeper interactions. Every one diluted the pair (all < 1.020) \u2014 good evidence there were no hidden complementary signals and that the strong singles carry the actual information.\n4. **Sign verification:** flipping any of the three signs collapsed the Sharpe (1.142 \u2192 0.53/0.49/0.13), so the orientation is unambiguous rather than noise-fitted.\n\n### Reasoning behind the forecast\nI deliberately predicted well below the 1.142 in-sample figure. With n=5000, SE on an annualized Sharpe is \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22, and I took the maximum over ~85 evaluations \u2014 though most shared features 27/17, so the effective number of independent tries is closer to 10\u201320, implying ~0.15\u20130.30 of selection inflation. Layered on top is regime drift, which in practice typically dominates pure sampling error.\n\nTwo things argue *against* heavy shrinkage, which is why I landed at 0.80 rather than lower: the winner is composed of the three best standalone features (each many SE from zero, so genuinely predictive rather than mined noise), and the combination gain is explainable by ordinary diversification. The 0.30 SD spans roughly 0.2\u20131.4, honestly covering both material decay and the signal holding up."}
